{
  "result": "There's a specific kind of quiet that only exists at 5:47 a.m. It's not quite night, not quite morning. The world hasn't decided to turn on yet. \n\nMy coffee maker clicks, hisses, and then sighs. That sigh is the most honest sound in my apartment. It doesn't pretend to be excited about the day. It's just doing its job, hot water through dark grounds, same as yesterday. \n\nI think a lot about how we collect things without meaning to. Not souvenirs or stamps, but phrases. Someone once told me 'you're burning the candle at both ends, but you're saving the middle for someone who'll never show up.' I didn't write it down, but I kept it. It lives in my head rent-free next to the jingle for a toothpaste commercial from 2003 and the exact way my mom said 'well, that's that' when she finally fixed the leaky faucet.\n\nWe like to think we're building these grand narratives, arcs and climaxes. Mostly, we're just accumulating small, warm details. The chipped blue mug that is objectively the best mug. The dog two floors down who barks only when the elevator dings. The way late afternoon light makes dust look intentional, like it's supposed to be there, floating. \n\nMaybe that's enough. Not every moment needs to mean something bigger. Maybe sometimes a good cup of coffee at an in-between hour is the whole point. And honestly, if I get to sit here a little longer before the world turns on, I will.",
